      "text": "Part 5 - Linux Filesystem",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Part 5 - Linux Filesystem",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "r5ZDa-AnEEPKftvXHuvrO",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1913.0561296076967,
      "y": 2198.507779093081,
      "width": 814,
      "height": 1000,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGR",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 844538124,
      "version": 50,
      "versionNonce": 537875636,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785165902631,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Everything starts from one directory: /\nThis is called the root directory, Think of it as the root of a tree.\n\n                /\n        ───────┼────────\n        │      │       │\n      home    etc     var\n        │\n      amit\n        │\n    Projects\n\n\nEvery file and directory exists somewhere under /.\n\n\nImportant Directories\n\n| Directory | Purpose                                           |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/`       | Root of the filesystem                            |\n| `/home`   | User home directories                             |\n| `/root`   | Home directory of the root (administrator) user   |\n| `/etc`    | System configuration files                        |\n| `/var`    | Variable data such as logs, caches, and databases |\n| `/tmp`    | Temporary files                                   |\n| `/usr`    | User applications and libraries                   |\n| `/bin`    | Essential system commands                         |\n| `/opt`    | Optional third-party software                     |\n\n\nWhy does this matter?\n\nLater we will see things like:\n/var/log/nginx/\n/etc/nginx/\n/etc/systemd/\n/usr/bin/python3\n\nKnowing the directory structure helps you troubleshoot production systems.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Everything starts from one directory: /\nThis is called the root directory, Think of it as the root of a tree.\n\n                /\n        ───────┼────────\n        │      │       │\n      home    etc     var\n        │\n      amit\n        │\n    Projects\n\n\nEvery file and directory exists somewhere under /.\n\n\nImportant Directories\n\n| Directory | Purpose                                           |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/`       | Root of the filesystem                            |\n| `/home`   | User home directories                             |\n| `/root`   | Home directory of the root (administrator) user   |\n| `/etc`    | System configuration files                        |\n| `/var`    | Variable data such as logs, caches, and databases |\n| `/tmp`    | Temporary files                                   |\n| `/usr`    | User applications and libraries                   |\n| `/bin`    | Essential system commands                         |\n| `/opt`    | Optional third-party software                     |\n\n\nWhy does this matter?\n\nLater we will see things like:\n/var/log/nginx/\n/etc/nginx/\n/etc/systemd/\n/usr/bin/python3\n\nKnowing the directory structure helps you troubleshoot production systems.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
